#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "e16376f2-ffb9-580c-a7e7-57f5814f8289" :question/math-academy-id "e-21137" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}[math]\\phantom{X \\mid}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\phantom{X \\mid}[/math]\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3c_{1} + 2c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} - c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{3c_{1} + 2c_{2} = 7; c_{1} - c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "d2768264-6d1a-5e62-9af4-025c877e138b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5608" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t)$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1= 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2= -3$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =2$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =-3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}v_{1}e^{λ_{1}t} + c_{2}v_{2}e^{λ_{2}t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} + 6c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + c_{2} = 5; c_{1} + 6c_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_{2} =-5⟹c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nc_{1} - 1 = 5⟹c_{1} = 6\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 6\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "226a5f8f-ac26-5f3e-8f8f-f145c314820f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5609" :question/problem "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ -2c_{1} - 3c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{6c_{1} + c_{2} =-11; - 2c_{1} - 3c_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1=-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= -2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3 \\cdot 2} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12e^{6} + e^{-4} \\\\ 4e^{6} - 3e^{-4}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903e607e-bfee-5720-bbf1-dae696d71f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131111} {:answer/id #uuid "a24ca47b-bc24-508f-bbd2-9ae6c2c227a0" :answer/typ